GET /api/wine/story.body:
{
  "title": "Zero to One: How Wine Comes Into Being",
  "essence": "From wild vine to poured glass — eight thousand years of humans learning to let sunlight, stored as sugar and handed to yeast, become something greater than the sum of grape and time.",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "first-jar",
      "title": "The First Jar",
      "period": "c. 6000 BCE",
      "story": "In the South Caucasus — modern Georgia — Neolithic farmers buried clay vessels in the earth and let crushed grapes ferment inside them. Chemical traces in these qvevri are the oldest known evidence of winemaking, roughly eight thousand years old. Before the wheel found its axle, wine had found its jar."
    },
    {
      "id": "two-strangers",
      "title": "Two Strangers Meet",
      "period": "biology, always",
      "story": "Wine needs only two things to begin: ripe fruit and hungry yeast. Vitis vinifera stores a summer of sunlight as sugar, and wild Saccharomyces, riding on the grape's own skin, eats that sugar and breathes out alcohol. Nobody invented fermentation. Humans only noticed it, and learned not to interrupt."
    },
    {
      "id": "wine-dark-sea",
      "title": "The Wine-Dark Sea",
      "period": "c. 3000 BCE – 400 CE",
      "story": "Egypt pressed grapes for its priests, Greece gave the vine a god in Dionysus, and Rome carried both across an empire in sealed clay amphorae. Wine became trade, sacrament and daily bread at once. Where the legions marched, the vineyards followed."
    },
    {
      "id": "patient-orders",
      "title": "The Patient Orders",
      "period": "c. 900 – 1400",
      "story": "Through the Middle Ages, Benedictine and Cistercian monks in Burgundy kept the vine alive and studied it plot by plot. Tasting the difference one wall could make, they gave the world the idea of terroir — that place has a flavour. They were the first to believe the ground remembers."
    },
    {
      "id": "the-ranking",
      "title": "The Ranking",
      "period": "1855",
      "story": "For the Paris Exposition Universelle, on the order of Napoleon III, Bordeaux's brokers ranked the great châteaux by the prices their wines commanded. The 1855 Classification named the First Growths — Lafite, Latour, Margaux and Haut-Brion — and it still governs prestige today. A hierarchy of hillsides, written down and never quite erased."
    },
    {
      "id": "root-louse",
      "title": "The Root Louse",
      "period": "1863 – 1890s",
      "story": "A tiny American aphid, phylloxera, reached Europe and devoured the roots of nearly every vine on the continent. The cure was humbling: graft the noble European grape onto tough American rootstock, which resists the pest. Almost every vine in Europe now grows on borrowed roots."
    },
    {
      "id": "invisible-workers",
      "title": "The Invisible Workers",
      "period": "1857 – 1866",
      "story": "Louis Pasteur proved that fermentation is not chance but life — the work of living yeast — and that spoilage is the work of other microbes. His Études sur le vin turned winemaking from craft into a science that could be steered. He gave winemakers eyes to see what had always been at work."
    },
    {
      "id": "judgment-of-paris",
      "title": "The Judgment of Paris",
      "period": "1976 – now",
      "story": "In a 1976 blind tasting in Paris, French judges ranked California wines above famous Bordeaux and Burgundy, and did not know it until the envelopes opened. The result broke the assumption that great wine could come from only one place. Today great wine comes from six continents, and the vine keeps moving with the climate."
    }
  ]
}

