GET /api/cigar/story.body:
{
  "title": "From Seed to Smoke — the zero to one of the cigar",
  "essence": "A cigar is a plant taught to burn slowly. The gap between a green leaf and a gray ash is five years wide. Everything in between is the craft.",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "before-the-name",
      "title": "Before the name",
      "period": "before 1492",
      "story": "Tobacco is American. For thousands of years the peoples of the Caribbean and Mesoamerica grew it, dried it, and burned it as ritual — a carved relief at Palenque shows a Maya figure drawing smoke from a rolled tube. The Taíno of Cuba called their rolled leaves by a word the world would inherit: cohiba. Smoke was not leisure. It was prayer made visible."
    },
    {
      "id": "first-contact",
      "title": "First contact",
      "period": "1492",
      "story": "In November 1492, on Cuba, two of Columbus's crew — Rodrigo de Jerez and Luis de Torres — watched Taíno people drink smoke from burning rolls of leaf. Jerez carried the habit home to Spain, and the story goes the Inquisition jailed him for it: only the devil, they reasoned, gives a man the power to exhale smoke. Two worlds met in a single breath. The cigar was the bridge."
    },
    {
      "id": "seville-and-smoke",
      "title": "Seville and smoke",
      "period": "1500s–1700s",
      "story": "Spain made tobacco a crown monopoly and funneled Cuban leaf through Seville, where the great Royal Tobacco Factory rose in the eighteenth century — later the setting of Bizet's Carmen. Around 1560 the French diplomat Jean Nicot sent leaf to the court of France; nicotine still carries his name. Europe learned to smoke before it learned why."
    },
    {
      "id": "havana-rises",
      "title": "Havana rises",
      "period": "1817–1900",
      "story": "In 1817 a royal decree ended the monopoly, and Cuba was free to roll and sell its own. The great houses followed: H. Upmann in 1844, Partagás in 1845, Hoyo de Monterrey in 1865, Romeo y Julieta in 1875. From 1865, lectores read novels and news aloud to the rollers at their benches — Dumas was a favorite, and one factory's cigars took the name Montecristo. The golden age of Havana was a factory full of listening hands."
    },
    {
      "id": "exile-and-embargo",
      "title": "Exile and embargo",
      "period": "1959–1962",
      "story": "The revolution of 1959 nationalized the factories, and in 1962 the United States embargoed the island — though President Kennedy first sent Pierre Salinger to gather some 1,200 H. Upmann petit coronas the night before he signed. The great families scattered: to the Dominican Republic, to Honduras, to Nicaragua, to the Canary Islands, carrying seed and memory. The gap between homeland and craft opened wide. The exiles rolled across it."
    },
    {
      "id": "the-new-world",
      "title": "The new world answers",
      "period": "1960s–1990s",
      "story": "In exile the craft took new root: Padrón in Miami from 1964, Joya de Nicaragua in Estelí from 1968, the Fuentes rebuilding in the Dominican Republic. The boom of the 1990s — Cigar Aficionado launched in 1992 — remade the industry, and in 1995 Fuente Fuente OpusX proved a great wrapper could grow in Dominican soil. Cuba was no longer the only answer. It became one answer among several true ones."
    },
    {
      "id": "the-leaf-now",
      "title": "The leaf now",
      "period": "2000s–present",
      "story": "Nicaragua's volcanic valleys now stand beside Vuelta Abajo in the reckoning of the world's best leaf, and small factories treat blending the way winemakers treat vineyards. A cigar still cannot be hurried: seed to smoke remains a matter of years. In an accelerating world, the cigar is a deliberate slowness. The leaf is."
    }
  ]
}

