GET /api/cigar/process.body:
{
  "essence": "From a seed smaller than a grain of sand to a finished cigar is a matter of years. Nothing in the chain can be rushed; every stage is a form of waiting done correctly.",
  "stages": [
    {
      "id": "seed",
      "stage": "seed",
      "title": "The seedbed",
      "what": "Tobacco seeds — thousands fit in a spoon — are germinated in protected seedbeds and trays for about 45 days until the seedlings can stand the field.",
      "why": "Everything the cigar will become is already in this dust. The grower's first job is simply to keep the future alive."
    },
    {
      "id": "field",
      "stage": "field",
      "title": "The field",
      "what": "Seedlings are transplanted and grown for two to three months — in full sun for filler and dark wrapper, or under cloth tents (tapado) for fine shade wrapper.",
      "why": "Sun thickens the leaf and loads it with oils and strength; shade keeps it thin, elastic, and even. The light decides the leaf's destiny."
    },
    {
      "id": "harvest",
      "stage": "harvest",
      "title": "The priming",
      "what": "Leaves are picked by hand, two or three at a time, from the bottom of the plant upward as each level ripens — volado low, seco in the middle, ligero at the top.",
      "why": "Position is character: the highest leaves take the most sun and carry the most power. A plant is harvested the way a story is read — in order."
    },
    {
      "id": "curing",
      "stage": "curing",
      "title": "The curing barn",
      "what": "Leaves are sewn in pairs and hung on poles in the casa de tabaco, air-curing for 45 to 60 days as green turns gold, then brown.",
      "why": "Curing removes water and chlorophyll. The leaf dies slowly and deliberately, which is the only way it can be useful."
    },
    {
      "id": "fermentation",
      "stage": "fermentation",
      "title": "The pilón",
      "what": "Cured leaves are moistened and stacked into pilones — dense piles that heat themselves from within — then torn down, turned, and rebuilt, again and again over months.",
      "why": "Fermentation is what makes tobacco smokable: harshness and ammonia leave, aroma arrives. This is the cigar's true birth."
    },
    {
      "id": "aging",
      "stage": "aging",
      "title": "The sleep",
      "what": "Fermented leaf is baled — traditionally in palm-bark tercios — and rested in aging rooms for months or years before anyone rolls it.",
      "why": "Time rounds what fire and heat began. Great factories sit on warehouses of sleeping leaf the way banks sit on gold."
    },
    {
      "id": "sorting",
      "stage": "sorting",
      "title": "The despalillo",
      "what": "Workers strip the central stem from each leaf and grade the leaf by size, color, and texture — wrapper apart from binder, binder apart from filler.",
      "why": "A cigar is built from chosen leaves, not available ones. Sorting is where standards become physical."
    },
    {
      "id": "blending",
      "stage": "blending",
      "title": "The blend",
      "what": "The master blender writes the recipe: how much ligero for strength, seco for aroma, volado for an even burn, and which binder and wrapper complete it.",
      "why": "No single leaf is a cigar. The blend is the composition — the difference between noise and music."
    },
    {
      "id": "rolling",
      "stage": "rolling",
      "title": "The torcedor",
      "what": "At the bench, filler is bunched into the binder, pressed in molds, then dressed in the wrapper and finished — in the Cuban manner — with a triple cap.",
      "why": "Years of agriculture pass through two human hands in a few minutes. The roller is where the leaf becomes the object."
    },
    {
      "id": "resting",
      "stage": "resting",
      "title": "The marriage",
      "what": "Finished cigars rest in the escaparate — the cool cedar aging room — until their moistures equalize and the blend settles; then they are color-sorted, banded, and boxed.",
      "why": "A just-rolled cigar is an argument still in progress. Rest lets the leaves agree with each other before the fire asks its question."
    }
  ]
}

GET /api/cigar/selection.body:
{
  "essence": "Choosing a cigar is reading a leaf before the fire does. Look, touch, and be honest about the hour you have.",
  "guide": [
    {
      "factor": "Wrapper",
      "how": "Look for even color, a faint oily sheen, and fine veins — no cracks, tears, or dry patches. The wrapper is a large share of the flavor and all of the first impression."
    },
    {
      "factor": "Construction",
      "how": "Pinch gently along the body: it should give evenly, with no soft voids or hard knots. A well-made cigar feels consistent from foot to head."
    },
    {
      "factor": "Draw",
      "how": "A good draw pulls like sipping through a straw — neither a fight nor a free fall. Too tight will not smoke; too loose will burn hot and fast."
    },
    {
      "factor": "Size for the hour",
      "how": "A robusto asks about 45 minutes; a Churchill wants ninety or more. Choose the cigar to fit the time you actually have — a rushed cigar is a wasted one."
    },
    {
      "factor": "Strength versus body",
      "how": "Strength is nicotine; body is the weight of flavor; they are not the same. Start mild-to-medium and climb as your palate learns the map."
    },
    {
      "factor": "Storage",
      "how": "Keep cigars in a humidor at 65–70% relative humidity and around 65–70°F. Too dry, it burns hot and bitter; too wet, it draws badly and risks mold. A cigar is alive until it is lit."
    },
    {
      "factor": "Source",
      "how": "Buy from reputable merchants. Counterfeit Cubans are an industry of their own; provenance is part of the purchase."
    },
    {
      "factor": "Rest",
      "how": "A cigar that has just traveled is out of balance. Let it rest in your humidor for a few weeks before lighting — the blend re-settles, and the cigar you smoke is the one the blender intended."
    }
  ]
}

GET /api/cigar/rituals.body:
{
  "essence": "A ritual is the arbitrage between burning a cigar and smoking one. The cigar is slow on purpose; the ceremony is the point.",
  "rites": [
    {
      "id": "the-inspection",
      "name": "The inspection and the cold draw",
      "how": "Before any flame, look the wrapper over and pinch gently along the body for soft spots or hard knots. Then cut, and draw on the unlit cigar — the cold draw — testing the resistance and tasting the raw leaf.",
      "why": "The cold draw is the only preview the fire allows. It confirms the draw is right and gives the first honest taste of the blend."
    },
    {
      "id": "the-cut",
      "name": "The cut",
      "how": "Choose the instrument: the guillotine takes the whole face, the V-cut a clean wedge, the punch a small round opening. Whatever the tool, cut above the line of the cap — at the shoulder, never below it.",
      "why": "The cap is the only thing anchoring the wrapper to the head. Cut below its line and the cigar can unravel in your hand."
    },
    {
      "id": "toasting-the-foot",
      "name": "Toasting the foot",
      "how": "Hold the foot near the flame without letting it touch, rotating until the rim glows evenly. Only then bring the cigar to your lips and draw the fire home.",
      "why": "An even start is an even burn. A cigar rushed into flame chars one side and runs, and the first minutes are spent correcting instead of tasting."
    },
    {
      "id": "never-inhale",
      "name": "Never inhale",
      "how": "Draw the smoke into the mouth, let it rest on the palate, and release it. Experienced tasters sometimes push a little out through the nose — the retrohale — to read the aroma.",
      "why": "Cigar smoke is not meant for the lungs. The palate and the nose are the instruments; everything the blender composed is legible there."
    },
    {
      "id": "the-ash",
      "name": "The ash",
      "how": "Let the ash build to an inch or so, then roll or tap it gently into the tray before it falls on its own. A cigar is never flicked like a cigarette.",
      "why": "A firm, long ash is the signature of well-rolled long filler. Tradition holds that it also shields the coal and keeps the burn cool — lore, but lore that generations of tasters keep repeating."
    },
    {
      "id": "the-band",
      "name": "The band question",
      "how": "Leave the band on until the cigar has warmed well into its smoking; then, if you choose to remove it, slide it off gently. If it resists, leave it be.",
      "why": "The etiquette — old custom read a displayed band as boasting — is tradition, not law. The practical part is real: heat softens the band's gum, and a warm band comes away without tearing the wrapper."
    },
    {
      "id": "the-three-thirds",
      "name": "The three thirds",
      "how": "Read the cigar in thirds: the first introduces the blend, the second develops it, and the final third is the strongest and darkest.",
      "why": "Blenders compose cigars to evolve, and the smoke drawn through the remaining leaf carries flavor and strength forward as it shortens. The end of a cigar is always more concentrated than its beginning."
    },
    {
      "id": "the-resting-pace",
      "name": "The resting pace",
      "how": "Set the cigar down in the tray between draws and let it wait — about a minute is the customary rhythm.",
      "why": "Drawing too often overheats the coal, and a hot cigar turns bitter. Rested, it stays cool and keeps telling the truth."
    },
    {
      "id": "the-dignified-end",
      "name": "The dignified end",
      "how": "When you are finished, do not stub or crush the cigar. Lay it in the ashtray and let it go out on its own.",
      "why": "Left alone, a cigar dies quietly within a few minutes; ground out, it releases its harshest smoke. Tradition adds that a thing which took years to make has earned an unhurried end."
    },
    {
      "id": "the-relight",
      "name": "The relight",
      "how": "A cigar that has gone out may be relit within a few hours without shame. First purge it — blow gently outward through the cigar to expel the stale smoke — then knock off the loose ash and toast the foot as if for the first time.",
      "why": "Stale smoke and tars gather at a dead coal. The purge and a fresh toast recover most of what the cigar was; leave it until tomorrow and less of it will answer."
    }
  ]
}

