      "geography": "Eastern Mediterranean — Phoenicia (Tyre, Sidon) gave it its name, but the earliest production is Minoan (Crete, Kythera, Chryssi)."
    },
    "production": "The colourless precursor is extracted from the snail's hypobranchial gland (large snails milked individually; small ones crushed whole). On exposure to air and sunlight it oxidises through yellow-green to the final purple. Historically the glands were salted, then macerated and gently heated/fermented in vats for days to develop and fix the dye. Famously wasteful: about 12,000 Bolinus brandaris yield only ~1.4 g of pure dye. First laboratory synthesis reported 1903 (Sachs & Kempf), with a second route in 1904; Friedländer determined the structure in 1909 and confirmed the synthetic compound was identical to ancient Tyrian purple; it has never been produced commercially, though recent work biosynthesises it in engineered bacteria.",
    "chemistry": {
      "composition": "An organic (animal-derived) vat dye: predominantly 6,6'-dibromoindigo, accompanied in genuine shellfish purple by 6-bromoindigo, indigo and mono-/di-brominated indirubin isomers. The bromine content is the diagnostic fingerprint distinguishing true murex purple from plant/lichen imitations.",
      "formula": "C16H8Br2N2O2"
    },
    "dates": {
      "first_attested": {
        "year": -1750,
        "text": "Earliest evidence is Minoan, not Phoenician: murex-processing installations and crushed-shell middens on Crete and nearby islets (e.g. Chryssi, inhabited c. 1800-1500 BCE; Kouphonisi/Palaikastro deposits dated c. 2000-1600 BCE) point to sea-purple manufacture before c. 1750 BCE. Phoenician tradition follows (variously cited c. 1570 or c. 1200 BCE); the dyed Timna textiles date c. 1000 BCE. Given competing archaeological ranges, source-declared.",
        "source_url": "https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.2307/3210395",
        "truth_status": "source-declared"
      },
      "synthetic_from": {
        "year": 1903,
        "text": "6,6'-dibromoindigo was first synthesised in the laboratory by Sachs & Kempf in 1903 (a second route by Sachs & Sechel followed in 1904). Paul Friedländer's 1909 work determined the structure and confirmed the synthetic compound was identical to ancient Tyrian purple — it did not first make it. It was never synthesised at commercial scale, so any pre-1903 occurrence is necessarily natural murex extract.",
        "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6236399/",
        "truth_status": "verified"
      },
      "obsolescence": {
        "year": 1453,
        "text": "Murex-purple production was continued by Greeks, Romans and Byzantines until 1453 CE and the Ottoman fall of Constantinople; the imperial dyeworks had already been crippled by the 1204 sack, after which no ruler could sustain the cost. The craft then effectively disappeared, and later 'purple' was made from cheaper plant/insect sources.",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrian_purple",
        "truth_status": "source-declared"
      }
    },
    "lightfastness": "Exceptionally colour-fast for an organic colorant — famously it did not fade but grew brighter with weathering and sunlight, and dyed cloth was said to improve with washing. This wash- and light-fastness is unusual among natural dyes and part of why it was prized.",
    "toxicity": "The dye itself is non-toxic. Production, however, was notoriously foul: vast quantities of rotting molluscs made dye-works stink so badly they were sited downwind of towns.",
    "storage": "As a textile dye it is remarkably stable and light-fast; principal risks to dyed objects are the usual ones for organic material — high humidity, mould, and physical degradation of the fibre substrate rather than of the colorant. On manuscripts (the purple codices) and as a lake pigment it is more vulnerable to strong alkalinity and abrasion. Genuine murex purple is best confirmed non-destructively via its brominated-indigoid signature (e.g. HPLC-MS, Raman, XRF bromine).",
    "stories": [
      {
        "text": "Reserved for royalty and emperors and enforced by law: Roman sumptuary rules restricted true purple, and Byzantine heirs born to a reigning emperor were 'porphyrogenitus' ('born in the purple'). By late antiquity it was, weight for weight, worth more than gold.",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrian_purple",
        "truth_status": "verified"
      },
      {
        "text": "The staggering yield — roughly 12,000 Bolinus brandaris snails for ~1.4 g of pure dye (as quantified by Friedländer in 1909) — explains both the price and the mountains of discarded shells around ancient Tyre.",
        "source_url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6264235/",
        "truth_status": "verified"
      },
      {
        "text": "Origin legend: the god Melqart/Herakles' dog bit a sea snail on the beach near Tyre and stained its mouth purple, revealing the dye. A charming aetiological myth, not history.",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrian_purple",
        "truth_status": "contested"
      }
    ],
    "anachronism_marker": {
      "is_marker": true,
      "note": "Strong but qualified marker: it is primarily a dye, occurring on textiles/manuscripts more than in easel paint. Genuine murex purple's use spans c. 1750 BCE to 1453 CE, so it points to an ancient-through-Byzantine origin; crucially, its bromine (dibromoindigo) signature distinguishes real shellfish purple from cheaper madder/lichen/orchil imitations of any era. Since it was never made synthetically before 1903, brominated indigoid = authentic pre-modern purple."
    },
    "sources": [
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrian_purple",
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/6,6%27-Dibromoindigo",
      "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6264235/",
